WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.080
One protein pulls some growing axons toward the midline of the spinal cord.

2
00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:10.060
It pushes others away.

3
00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:11.980
What decides?

4
00:00:12.283 --> 00:00:17.223
A growing axon is led by its growth cone, the moving, sensing tip at its end.

5
00:00:17.767 --> 00:00:23.527
Now the map. Picture the embryonic spinal cord cut crosswise, like a slice through a

6
00:00:23.527 --> 00:00:24.400
tube.

7
00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:26.240
the bottom sits the floor plate.

8
00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:29.540
It is a strip of cells along the ventral midline.

9
00:00:30.133 --> 00:00:32.913
The ventricular zone lines the central canal.

10
00:00:33.873 --> 00:00:37.753
Its cells are progenitors, dividing cells that make neurons and glia.

11
00:00:38.333 --> 00:00:42.373
Each progenitor sends a long fibre outward to the edge of the cord.

12
00:00:43.017 --> 00:00:45.017
That outer edge is the pial surface.

13
00:00:45.917 --> 00:00:50.077
A basement membrane covers it, a thin sheet of extracellular matrix.

14
00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:53.710
Many commissural neurons sit in the dorsal cord.

15
00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:57.370
Their axons grow ventrally, close to the pial surface.

16
00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:01.630
Then they turn inward to the floor plate and cross it to the other side.

17
00:01:02.283 --> 00:01:04.683
Add a second traveller, on its own map.

18
00:01:05.483 --> 00:01:08.263
This is the neural tube where midbrain meets hindbrain.

19
00:01:09.183 --> 00:01:14.323
There, trochlear motor axons start near the ventral midline and grow dorsally, away

20
00:01:14.323 --> 00:01:15.323
from the floor plate.

21
00:01:15.767 --> 00:01:22.187
Add a third. In the worm C. elegans, cells and pioneer axons migrate between the

22
00:01:22.187 --> 00:01:23.487
ventral and dorsal sides.

23
00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:28.290
The worm's netrin, called UNC-6, is made on the ventral side.

24
00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:30.520
The map raises three puzzles.

25
00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:36.440
First, a floor plate in a gel draws axons from dorsal cord without touching it.

26
00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:38.080
What crosses?

27
00:01:38.433 --> 00:01:43.753
Second, how can one cue pull commissural axons toward the ventral midline, and push

28
00:01:43.753 --> 00:01:45.053
trochlear axons away?

29
00:01:45.683 --> 00:01:50.323
Third, is the cue a gradient from the midline, or laid along the axons' path?

30
00:01:50.917 --> 00:01:54.477
Make a guess. Is the direction written into the cue?

31
00:01:55.277 --> 00:01:56.937
Or does the cell that reads it decide?

32
00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:04.560
This lesson argues for the second.

33
00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:06.600
Netrin marks a place.

34
00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:09.320
The growth cone decides what to do there.

35
00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:16.970
Where netrin is supplies the where: its source, its slope, the surface it lies on.

36
00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:19.360
The growth cone supplies the sign.

37
00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:21.580
Its receptors come first.

38
00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:24.360
DCC usually draws it in.

39
00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:28.760
UNC5 receptors add repulsion, sometimes with DCC.

40
00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:35.590
In some cells, its internal signalling state counts too, such as cAMP or calcium.

41
00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:40.310
Laminin, a matrix protein outside the cell, can change that state.

42
00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:42.200
The rule has limits.

43
00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:45.320
Netrin is not always a long-range gradient.

44
00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:47.340
And it does more than steer.

45
00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:51.010
Keep this map in view.

46
00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:53.750
Each chapter fills one of the three boxes.
